## Conversion to Iter — the universal iteration entry point.
##
## Any type that implements `ToIter` can be iterated: its elements are
## available to all Iter combinators (filter, map, fold, etc.) via a
## single `next` method that returns `Option 'elem`.
##
## Collections like Seq and List implement `ToIter` for generic code,
## but keep their own optimized inherent HOFs for direct use. When the
## fusion pass sees a concrete type at monomorphization time it can
## specialize to the direct-storage path — the ability is the generic
## interface, specialization recovers the fast path.
##
## Effectful producers (file handles, DB cursors) will implement
## `ToIter` once effect rows on ability methods are supported.
## For now `next` is pure — effectful iteration uses the descriptor
## tree's `CallbackProducerNode` directly.

use std::option

ability ToIter for 'c having 'elem where {
  next: { it: 'c } -> Option 'elem
}
